course: dictionary
lesson: 5187
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Jab is a good example. It can mean a quick poke, a fast punch, an injection, or a short unkind remark. In «a jab at her spelling», the words spelling and at give the clue: it is an unkind remark.
explain: Some meanings of jab are informal, which means they are used in everyday talk rather than in formal writing. In Britain, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, a jab is often an injection. «You can tell the nurse you are scared» sits near the words vaccination and nurse, so the clues point to an injection.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence. «She gave the cushion a jab with her finger» still makes sense with the meaning poke. If your meaning makes the sentence silly, try another meaning from the list.
fact: Jab can be a verb (you jab) or a noun (a jab).
fact: To jab is to push something pointed quickly and hard into or at something, like a finger or a stick.
fact: In boxing, a jab is a fast, straight punch.
fact: Informally, in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, a jab is an injection, like «the flu jab».
fact: Informally, «a jab at her spelling» is a short unkind remark.
exercise: «She gave the cushion a jab with her finger to plump it up.» What does jab mean here? | To wash very carefully | To sleep for a long time | To cry loudly | *To push something pointed quickly into something
exercise: The nurse says, «This jab will sting for a second, then the vaccine protects you.» What is a jab here? | A pointed stick | A fast punch | A rude remark | *An injection
exercise: «The boxer threw a fast jab, then a hook.» What is a jab in boxing? | A deep cut | A slow, gentle wave | A kind word | *A fast, straight punch
test: «Tetanus germs live in soil and can get into deep or dirty wounds, so nurses ask whether you have had a tetanus jab.» What does jab mean here? | A sort of soil | A cuddle | A sharp, rusty nail | *An injection that protects you from a dangerous germ
test: Her teacher gave a small jab: «Your spelling needs work.» What is a jab here? | A quick poke with a pencil | A kind compliment | *A short unkind remark | A fast boxing punch
exam: «The nurse gave me a jab, and it stung for a second.» Which meaning fits best? | A fast, straight punch | A short unkind remark | *An injection | A quick, hard poke with a finger
